Low-Power Edge AI Is Expanding FD-SOI Applications

Edge AI represents another important opportunity for the FD-SOI Foundry Market. Intelligent devices increasingly perform data processing directly within sensors, industrial equipment, connected devices and automotive systems.

Unlike centralized AI computing, edge applications often operate under strict power, thermal and physical constraints. FD-SOI can address these requirements through low-leakage transistor structures and adaptive body-bias techniques.

The technology is relevant to always-on sensing, voice processing, machine vision and industrial analytics. Its integration capabilities can also support systems combining digital processing, analog interfaces and wireless connectivity.

Embedded memory is further expanding the technology's potential. GlobalFoundries announced the availability of 22FDX+ embedded resistive random-access memory for prototyping in 2025, targeting secure wireless microcontrollers and AI/IoT system-on-chip applications.

These developments demonstrate how FD-SOI is evolving beyond traditional low-power semiconductor applications toward more integrated intelligent-edge platforms.

Embedded Memory and Advanced Process Nodes Are Strengthening the Technology Ecosystem

The commercial value of FD-SOI depends on more than transistor architecture. Customers also evaluate process design kits, intellectual property libraries, embedded memory, RF capabilities, automotive qualification and long-term manufacturing availability.

GlobalFoundries' 22FDX platform is an important example of an established commercial FD-SOI ecosystem. The platform supports applications involving automotive electronics, connectivity, IoT and edge computing.

STMicroelectronics is also expanding FD-SOI commercialization through its 18 nm technology roadmap. Its STM32V8 microcontroller incorporates next-generation FD-SOI technology with embedded phase-change memory, demonstrating the potential for higher-performance embedded computing applications.

The Crolles manufacturing expansion involving STMicroelectronics and GlobalFoundries further strengthens Europe's position in FD-SOI production.

The combination of process maturity, embedded memory and manufacturing capacity is becoming increasingly important as semiconductor customers evaluate long-term platform availability.

Wireless Connectivity and IoT Create Additional Demand

Wireless connectivity is another important application area for FD-SOI foundries. Connected devices require efficient processing, RF integration and reliable communication capabilities while maintaining low standby power consumption.

Battery-powered IoT products are particularly sensitive to energy requirements. Sensors, wireless microcontrollers and connected monitoring systems must operate for extended periods without frequent battery replacement.

FD-SOI can support these applications through low-leakage transistor operation and integration of RF and digital functions. Embedded memory options can further reduce the need for external components.

Industrial IoT is also creating opportunities for specialized semiconductor platforms. Connected manufacturing systems, smart sensors and automated equipment require long-lifecycle components that combine processing, communication and control capabilities.

These requirements support demand for foundry platforms that provide more than conventional digital logic manufacturing.

Manufacturing Capacity and Supply-Chain Diversification Remain Important

The geographic distribution of FD-SOI manufacturing is an important consideration for semiconductor customers.

GlobalFoundries supports FDX manufacturing through multi-site 300 mm operations in Malta, New York, and Dresden, Germany. Its Dresden facility represents a major European manufacturing base, while the company's U.S. operations support domestic supply-chain requirements.

The STMicroelectronics–GlobalFoundries Crolles expansion provides another important manufacturing development. The facility is designed for up to 620,000 300 mm wafers annually at full build-out.

For automotive, defense and industrial customers, manufacturing continuity is an important purchasing consideration. Semiconductor products used in these sectors often require long qualification periods and extended product availability.

Multi-site production can help reduce geographic concentration risks and improve supply assurance for customers operating across multiple regions.

